description = "Seven posts about building a Kubernetes platform on Hetzner, running it on real hardware until it stopped lying to me, and then arguing with myself about whether it was worth it. Everything measured rather than estimated, including the parts that did not come out in favour."
+++

I built a k3s platform for Hetzner, ran it on real hardware four times, and
wrote down what it cost. Then I wrote two posts arguing that you probably
should not do it.

Both halves are honest, which is the point. Most writing about self-hosting is
either a tutorial with the failures edited out, or a warning from someone who
never finished. This is neither: it is one platform, measured, with the bill and
the arguments against it in the same series.

The headline numbers, so you can decide whether to read further. Three nodes and
a load balancer cost **€122.45 a month**. The platform itself — delivery,
certificates, ingress, monitoring, upgrades — occupies about **4.5 GiB** before
any workload of yours runs, and that figure barely moves as you add tenants,
which is the entire argument for the shape. Getting there produced **49 written
lessons**, and almost every one of them is a thing that *reported success* while
being wrong.

## Where to start

**If you have not decided whether to do this at all**, read the two challenges
first — [three FreeBSD boxes](/blog/three-freebsd-boxes-instead-of-k3s/) and
[Docker Swarm](/blog/docker-swarm-instead/). They are the honest alternatives
and both of them beat Kubernetes for a real range of situations. If either one
convinces you, you have saved yourself the rest of the series and I would call
that a good outcome.

**If you have decided and want the thing**, start at [the kit
itself](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/) and read forward. Parts two and three are the
pieces people discover they needed afterwards.

**If you want to see it pointed at something**, the last two parts take two
awkward workloads — a game world server and a factory that runs code written by
a language model — and ask what actually fits in a cluster and what does not.

## The seven parts

1. **[K3s starter kit on Hetzner cloud](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/)** — what the
   platform is, what it costs, and the three failures that taught it the most.
   The one to read if you read only one.

2. **[OpenTelemetry on a self-hosted k3s
   cluster](/blog/opentelemetry-on-k3s/)** — why a collector sits between your
   applications and the stores. An application knows what it *is*; it does not
   know which pod it is running in, and that gap is the whole reason.

3. **[The edge of a self-hosted
   cluster](/blog/the-edge-of-a-self-hosted-cluster/)** — websockets against
   rolling deployments, what a cloud load balancer does to the client address,
   and whether to put a CDN in front of your own origin.

4. **[Three FreeBSD boxes instead of a k3s
   cluster](/blog/three-freebsd-boxes-instead-of-k3s/)** — the first argument
   against. I run both, and the money turns out not to be about the operating
   system at all.

5. **[Docker Swarm instead of a k3s
   platform](/blog/docker-swarm-instead/)** — the second argument against.
   Including whether Swarm is actually dead, which is mostly a case of two
   different products sharing a name.

6. **[What a game world server actually
   needs](/blog/what-a-game-world-server-needs/)** — split a game server
   honestly and almost all of it belongs in a cluster. Exactly one process does
   not, and it is the one that is the game.

7. **[A software factory on your own
   cluster](/blog/software-factory-on-your-own-cluster/)** — running code a
   language model wrote, at scale, on hardware you own. That is a tenancy
   problem before it is an AI problem.

## What ties them together

Every one of these was written against a cluster that existed. The cluster is
gone now — I tore it down and verified the account was empty — but the numbers
were taken while it was up, and where I could not measure something I say so
rather than estimating it.

That last habit is the most useful thing in the series, and it is why several
posts carry a section listing what they have **not** proven. A restore has never
been performed. No CDN has ever fronted this cluster. The game world server was
never profiled under load. Those absences are more informative than another
paragraph of confident prose would have been.

The platform is at
[hcloud-k3s-platform-kit](https://github.com/sasin91/hcloud-k3s-platform-kit),
MIT, with the reasoning attached to every decision and the lessons in a file of
their own. If you take one thing from any of this, take that file — it is the
part I would keep if I had to throw the rest away.
